<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sir Charles Wheler, 2nd Baronet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1620–<a href="/years/1683">1683</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Charles Wheler</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Charles Wheler, 2nd Baronet, was an English soldier and politician who sat in the House of Commons. In 1671 he became the first governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> after they were separated from Barbados. He quarrelled with the colonists and with the French on St Kitts over the return of English lands, and was recalled in 1672, being succeeded by Sir William Stapleton.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles Wheler was born around 1620, the only son of William Wheler of Martin Husingtree in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcestershire">Worcestershire</a> and Nantwich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>, and his wife Eleanor, daughter of Edward Polkston of Allington in county <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbigh">Denbigh</a>. He was a student at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> in 1638 and became a Fellow of Trinity College, Cambridge, and an M.A. of the university. At the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> he was among those entrusted with carrying the university's plate to Charles I to help pay for the Royalist cause, and he was ejected from his fellowship on 18 April 1644.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Wheler served as a major in a Royalist cavalry regiment from early in the war until 1644, then in a regiment of foot until early 1646, when he surrendered to Parliament and was allowed to go into exile. In 1656 he was commissioned as a captain in Charles II's Guards, then a regiment of the Spanish army, which fought at the Battle of the Dunes in 1658.</p><p>After the Restoration he was a Gentleman of the Privy Chamber from 1660 and a captain in the 1st Foot Guards from 1661 to 1672, and held commands in the Duke of Richmond's Horse and Prince Rupert's Horse. He succeeded his first cousin Sir William Wheler as 2nd Baronet on 6 August 1666, though without the first baronet's estates. He was Member of Parliament for the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> from 1667 to 1679 and governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> in 1671–1672. In 1678–1679 he was colonel of a regiment that later became the 7th Regiment of Foot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1676 he inherited the estate of Sir Thomas Trevor, 1st Baronet, his mother's first cousin, at Leamington Hastings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwickshire">Warwickshire</a>. He had married Dorothy, daughter of Sir Francis Bindlosse of Borwick-Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, on 7 August 1648. Their eldest son Trevor died in 1678, a major in his father's regiment, and another son, Sir Francis Wheler, became an admiral and drowned in a wreck on the rocks of Scilly. Sir Charles died on 26 August 1683, was buried at Leamington Hastings and was succeeded by his son Sir William Wheler, 3rd Baronet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Wheler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1671 the crown separated the Leeward Caribee Islands from Barbados and gave them their own governor-in-chief, first <b>Sir Charles Wheler</b> and then Sir William Stapleton.</blockquote><a href="/state/leeward-islands-colony">Leeward Islands colony</a></li></ul></section>
